WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.040
Pipe Dream.

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.120
It takes about five seconds to get the idea.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.420
It takes a lifetime to get good at it.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.080
Really good.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:32.440
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.300
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.520
And I'm Joe.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.500
Is that statement true?

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.140
That could have been just like the opening argument for the game, I guess.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.420
Like that's what you could have said outside of the back of the box, you know?

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.860
I feel like I've got to break down that last part though.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.520
Like it takes a lifetime to get

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.400
good. Really good.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.200
But it's saying really good like...

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.260
So it takes a lifetime just to get really

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.940
good. Or does it take a lifetime to get good?

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.400
Well yeah, I mean, it'll take you any amount of time to get good at it, but you should know

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:14.080
that any idea of you being good at the game isn't truly crystallized until a lifetime later.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.380
Huh. Interesting.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.100
Just so you know.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.840
Got it. What if you had a whole warehouse full of plumbing parts coming at you, and you had to build a

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.520
huge pipeline out of them?

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.240
What if there was a big puddle of slimy green stuff called flues,

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.240
and it was running through the pipes right behind you?

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.420
Well, that's the idea behind Pipe Dream.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.740
This is like the pitch meeting.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.280
That was actually an ad that they put out.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:47.020
And it has like a kid trying to like desperately

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:52.220
grab onto a pipe as he like falls down a chasm of pipes.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:57.680
It's funny. That was actually a thing that they had that is the least exciting for a kid.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.700
Right, right. It's making it look like a horror game.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:08.820
Yeah. No, it actually kind of sounded more like you were trying to make the game sound like ridiculous.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:16.300
Like I kind of understand that like the kid hanging from the pipe like that I can visualize in an ad,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.240
but it sounded more sarcastic the way you said it.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:21.460
Yes, that's true.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.100
You know, it took me less than five seconds to get the idea of this game.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.920
Do you know why that is? Because you played the game before?

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.260
Because I played this game before in, isn't this the hacking game in Bioshock?

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.740
Well, yeah. This whole thing has been reduced to a minigame in another video game?

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:44.280
Well, I mean, did you ever, I mean, like, you know, you had Windows as a kid.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.620
Did you play the Windows version of this game before Bioshock?

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.120
No, I only had, my Windows games were weird.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.220
There was a skiing game where the eventually the abominable snowman would eat you or whatever.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:03:00.500
And that was weird. I had a game where there was like mice.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:07.780
Yeah, you were the mouse and there were cats and you had to try to trap them while still being able to get the cheese.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.560
That one I'm not sure.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.880
That one sounds familiar to me a little bit.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.160
I feel like I had weird ones, too, that are not.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:18.580
Like, I had a pinball one, but not the one everyone talks about, like Galaxy Pinball or whatever.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:20.060
I think it's called Space Cadet.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:21.620
Is that what it's called?

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.260
That is the Windows XP built-in video game, yes.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.560
Yes, yeah. And obviously we all had Solitaire.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.460
Solitaire. We all had Solitaire.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.840
I think we all had Chips Challenge.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.520
Unless you didn't have Chips Challenge.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:37.640
Didn't have Chips Challenge.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:46.060
What's Chips Challenge? That was another bundled, like, just in the apps folder in the start menu of, like, Windows 98.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.360
On the computer, though, this wasn't known as Pipe Dream.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.420
It was known as Pipe Mania.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.920
But Pipe Dream is definitely the better name.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:01.620
Oh, yeah. I mean, Pipe Mania, I guess, like, that gets across the, like, wow, this is crazy.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.920
But Pipe Dream, that's just a perfect little play on words.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.120
Or maybe you'll have Pipe Mania.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.760
Like, you'll be dreaming. Oh, wait.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:08.980
I did it again. Pipe Dream.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.860
You'll be manic.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:13.800
Like, obsessive over the pipes.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.440
Hmm. That's funny.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:19.980
I thought I had it as Pipe Dream, but that could just be some Mandela shit.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.380
No, no. I mean, they might have been by that time.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.240
I'm just saying when it originally released on computers, because, you know, that's where it started.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.300
It was referred to as Pipe Mania.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:32.900
I think Pipe Dream is now just, you know, the way it's referred to in general.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.440
So I agree with you there.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.360
But apparently...

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:37.860
The Kleenex of Pipe Games.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:39.400
Yes. Yeah, yeah.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:46.140
And speaking of the Kleenex of Pipe Games, or just Pipe Games in general, I recognize this from Bioshock.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.660
But apparently this isn't, like, the only instance where it's, like, reduced to a...

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:54.980
Not necessarily a hacking thing, but, like, a minigame inside of a game.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.160
Apparently it's also in Saints Row 4, Warframe, Alien Swarm.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:01.720
I just find it interesting.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:02.940
Apparently Half-Life Alyx?

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.120
You were the one who played that, right, Sean?

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:07.080
Yeah, maybe I didn't get that far, though.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:17.440
I think that the codifier as, like, turning this into a minigame, or, like, the sort of stereotypical hacking minigame,

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.920
it was definitely Bioshock, though.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.120
I gotta say, I didn't have this on Windows.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:24.420
I've never played Bioshock.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:34.680
Not on my bingo card for this week's episode was that you both would be, like, pretty intimately familiar with this game from your childhoods.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:38.280
I just thought this was, like, the most out-there game that no one's ever heard of.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:39.280
Wow. Okay.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.740
We had very different experiences.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:44.400
I'm not that young, okay?

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.760
I played Bioshock, like, in my, you know, late teenage years.

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:52.460
Well, yeah, but I'm assuming the Windows version of it was when you were a kid, right?

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:56.100
Didn't have it. Oh, yeah, I'm the one that knows it from childhood.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.520
Oh, gotcha. So, Sean knows it from childhood.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:05.160
I kind of consider you both very youthful right now, so I consider this still the childhoods.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:07.520
I was just gonna say that we're representing all three generations.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:11.460
Sean is the childhood, I'm the teenager, and you're now learning it as an adult.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.800
So, we could talk about this game pretty confidently.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:19.060
We should probably just describe the game in case there is somebody out there who hasn't played Pipe Dream,

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:25.220
which wouldn't surprise me, even though, apparently, I have some familiarity with it.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:32.440
The game takes place on a grid where green slime flows from, like, the entry valve of the pipes.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:35.000
The one piece that, like, kind of isn't.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:36.280
Or white slime.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:38.500
Yeah. Oh, it's not green?

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:40.460
I think it's white.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:46.080
Okay. I assume that the pipes themselves are, like, those plastic spoons that change colors when liquid's on them,

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.400
and the slime inside the pipes is green.

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:50.960
Like the blood in your veins.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:52.680
It's not blue. Yeah.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:59.260
Anyway, so that slime starts at the beginning, and basically, it continues to flow,

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.300
so you have to continue to build pipes so that the slime doesn't just pour out.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:13.680
So, you have a selection of five different pipes with the bottom piece being the piece that you are putting into an empty square.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.280
New pieces continue to drop in from the top.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:25.320
So, you kind of have this Tetris-style rotation of, like, knowing what pieces are coming, but you have to be strategic about your placement because you might not need the current pipe.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:32.660
The goal is to create a continuous pipeline so that the slime can keep, or they're calling it flues in the game.

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.540
The flues. The floozies.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:36.020
Yeah. The floozies.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:38.000
The floozies can continue to flu or flow.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:45.300
And then, there is, like, a, for each stage, like, a minimum pipe length that you have to meet.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:56.960
It doesn't end after you hit that, so you can keep going for even more bonus points, but you basically have to hit, like, you know, make a pipe that's eight pieces long or 15 pieces long.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:01.760
It continues to get more challenging as the game goes on.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:18.960
Yeah. So, one thing to clarify, though, because you made the Tetris comparison, one might assume, especially if you've played sort of the, like, you know, the minigame version, that you can rotate.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.620
You cannot rotate anything.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:31.220
It's just, if you don't, if you have no use for this particular section, you just put it somewhere where, you know, you don't need it right now.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:35.980
Yeah, and I think that's a great distinction, but also, like, a necessary one for the game, right?

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.260
It would be kind of broken if it's just, you know, you just turn the—

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:42.260
Yeah, it would be kind of a boring game. Turn it at 90 degrees in any particular direction.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:53.740
It works in Tetris because the shape is still very specific, and so when you get the T block, there's still only so many ways you can place it as opposed to, you know, a turning pipe.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:56.620
You need it to turn in a very specific direction.

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:08.680
So now that we've laid down the rules, Joe, you know, since you're the one who didn't have any familiarity, did it take you five seconds to get the idea, and do you think you got good at it by the time of the recording of this podcast?

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:12.720
Yeah, it took me, you know, more or less five seconds to get the idea.

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:18.820
Maybe there might have been a couple things that I, like, realized a little later, but, like, yeah, I got what I was doing pretty quickly.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:28.880
I'll say, you know what, actually, the things that didn't come to me very easily just from playing it, I had to kind of, like, look up, was, like, am I trying to get this water off the screen?

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.280
Or this flues, sorry, off the screen?

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:34.480
Or, like, do I want it to go in at the longest distance possible?

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:37.160
Or, you know, which is—that's the case.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:38.160
You want the longer the distance.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:39.740
You're trying to match a certain distance.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.040
That's the one thing that, like, was not clear to me without, you know, kind of reading into it a little more.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:47.200
But, yeah, otherwise it was pretty easy to learn.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:48.420
As far as did I get good?

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.280
No. It is super unclear.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:10:00.080
Like, if there is a reason to keep going if you've reached that distance.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:04.800
Right. Because, yeah, in the game it doesn't tell you to do that.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.680
It's just, like, make sure it's this long.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:16.880
Um, I was kind of remembering, again, the Bioshock version, which has an outlet that you have to reach.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:21.400
Um, so I guess I kind of got colored by that past experience.

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:31.300
Um, but, yeah, you're just kind of, like, freestyling, trying to get the longest stuff, getting bonuses with loop-de-loops and all that.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.640
Um, it's kind of freeform.

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:49.740
And there's, like, a weird way of, like, the game, you know, dishing out score for placing the pipes where, um, basically you get points for, uh, the slime, the flues, uh, as it reaches the next pipe.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:51.120
You get points for that.

00:10:51.240 --> 00:11:00.920
But then you also, uh, lose points for any pieces of pipe that you have that you didn't use at all to, like, in the puzzle itself.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:02.900
Like, those go away and you lose points.

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:16.740
However, those points that you lose are a smaller amount or are a larger amount than if you just place a pipe over the pipe that you knew wasn't going to work.

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:26.360
And what I mean by that is, essentially, the game allows you to replace pipes, something that you can't do in a Tetris-style game, right?

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:29.100
You can't say, like, oh, I didn't mean to put that T-block there.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:31.840
I'm going to replace it with this L-shaped block, right?

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:39.240
In this game, you are able to kind of, like, go over your mistakes, provided you're still racing against this slime coming out.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:48.340
But it makes it a lot more manageable of, like, oh, I didn't get the piece I wanted, but it's fine because I can just kind of, like, place it over here for now and eventually just, like, fix it later.

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:55.260
Granted, that's a problem that only comes if you're able to, like, make it that long, but it still is just kind of, like, weird.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:12:08.260
It feels like it should be a little harder and you should have to, like, well, if you just get a pipe that sucks for you right now, it's like you just have to kind of, like, deal with that later and try to, like, make it work into your grander puzzle, not replace it.

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:20.980
Yeah, what I found myself doing, you know, at first it would be putting things where I might be able to use it later and then replace it if I need to.

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:30.880
But I will say that sometimes that did get me because sometimes I would be at a point where, like, okay, the floo's is almost to the end of my pipe so far and I'm racing against the clock.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:37.800
So then I would just start putting things down and replacing them over and over again to quickly cycle to the piece I need and lose a lot of points that way.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:38.820
So, like, the punishment is there.

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:41.920
Yeah, it does want you to be thoughtful as you lay your pipe.

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:45.560
What's that? Well, oh, sorry, I didn't.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.180
I just said it wants you to be thoughtful.

00:12:48.920 --> 00:13:00.060
Yeah, yeah. The most intricate part of any of these puzzles comes from when you have, like, the, like, depth pieces.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:02.700
So the pieces that kind of work on, like, two different...

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:03.620
The crossovers.

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:04.840
Yeah, the crossovers.

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:11.040
And so you can eventually make it so that the slime goes over previously filled panels but in a new direction.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:14.680
It's really satisfying when you do pull that off.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:20.960
I did manage to do it once in my playthrough and I really like just, like, you know, that's, like, oddly satisfying.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:23.240
When you're able to connect them in that way.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:36.220
I think the whole game kind of meets that oddly satisfying thing until it's not oddly satisfying because you suddenly can't, you know, like, you just can't do it fast enough or can't meet the requirement.

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:39.640
In which case it's very frustrating to watch the ooze beat you.

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:42.940
Yeah, I was...

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:49.500
Maybe this just comes from the decade more experience I apparently have with Pipe Dream.

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:52.220
But I was having fun with the crossovers.

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:54.900
You know, maybe more than once in my playthrough.

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:57.040
I'm better than you.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:58.780
Yeah, I like the crossovers.

00:13:58.900 --> 00:13:59.880
Don't get me wrong. I'm not...

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:01.460
I actually think every piece...

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:07.060
Every piece that they have in this game is interesting and enhances the game.

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:11.400
And that's to say that there are pipes other than just the different directions.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:16.380
There are pipes that, you know, can slow the...

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:20.780
You know, it takes, like, more time for the pipe to fill up.

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:22.360
So you get, like, a little extra...

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:23.640
A little reservoir.

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:30.460
Yeah. And then you have the pumps, which, you know, speed up the flows.

00:14:30.620 --> 00:14:31.980
You have to be careful about where you're placing those.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:36.800
Don't place those until you have kind of an area already built out in advance of them.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:42.880
You know, I think that's the kind of stuff that I was looking forward to as, like, as the game kept going on.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:46.080
Because it's not really a puzzle that you have to solve.

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:47.880
It's a puzzle that you're building, right?

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:50.980
This is a little different than other puzzle games we played on the NES.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:57.540
I guess what I mean by that is, like, Tetris has modes where, like, you know, you can just join in.

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:02.640
And it's like, here's a bunch of, you know, already destroyed blocks all over the screen, right?

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:03.980
And you have to, like, fill those out.

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:05.700
And that's not necessarily a puzzle either.

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:07.840
You're just dropping them down and it's a little more challenging.

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:12.360
Here, you're kind of getting the same thing where, like, you know, there's a start point.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:16.400
And you have to just kind of make this pipe go on for as long as possible.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:24.140
But then, like, as the game goes on, there are, like, you know, different mechanics are added to the game.

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:31.960
And different, like, the edges of the screen sometimes open up so you can connect one pipe all the way over to the other side.

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:33.920
Kind of, like, wrapping around the puzzle itself.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:40.520
Yeah. I think I get where this is more so a freeform puzzle game.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:41.980
It does not have a set solution.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:55.820
I think I prefer those in general to, like, a lot of the games that we've played that, like, you have that has the built-in reset button because of soft locking and all that.

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:59.100
Because they have a way that they want you to play the game.

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:00.900
And this does, too.

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:10.040
But it's more of a, like, kind of like a doodling kind of thing than a, what did they want me to do here?

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:12.600
Like, it just gives you a bunch of tools to play with.

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:14.020
Yeah, it's open.

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:16.620
And there's levels of success and failure.

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:18.160
You know, there's not just one answer.

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:20.040
It's kind of an open-world puzzle game.

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:22.520
Open-world puzzle game about laying pipe.

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:25.240
Right. Oh, I heard you.

